[stderr] # Runbook Generator
[stderr] 
[stderr] This repository is a generic, Supervisor-driven factory for turning a concise
[stderr] product brief into a complete, dependency-ordered implementation plan. It is
[stderr] not tied to a particular app type, framework, agent, or delivery platform.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Given a brief for a game, desktop application, web product, operating system,
[stderr] developer tool, service, document system, or any other product, the factory
[stderr] first discovers the product domains and creates a canonical specification. It
[stderr] then builds a scalable collection of detailed implementation runbooks.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Install Supervisor
[stderr] 
[stderr] Install the global Supervisor CLI once. It requires Python 3.10 or newer but
[stderr] does not require administrator access:
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```zsh
[stderr] curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jakowicz/supervisor/main/scripts/install.sh -o /tmp/supervisor-install.sh
[stderr] bash /tmp/supervisor-install.sh
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] If the installer says `~/.local/bin` is not on your `PATH`, add the line it
[stderr] prints to your shell profile, then open a new terminal. Confirm the commands
[stderr] are available:
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```zsh
[stderr] supervisor --help
[stderr] supervisor-run --help
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Start a factory run
[stderr] 
[stderr] From the repository root, there are only two actions required to start a
[stderr] factory run.
[stderr] 
[stderr] 1. Create a named project workspace and its
[stderr]    `projects/<project-name>/INITIAL.md` brief with the interactive wizard. It
[stderr]    asks for the project name, product type, target systems, users,
[stderr]    requirements, constraints, references, and open decisions. Responsive web
[stderr]    and PWA support are included by default.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```zsh
[stderr] supervisor initial --force
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr]    `--force` deliberately replaces an existing brief with the same project
[stderr]    name; omit it if you want the command to refuse to overwrite it.
[stderr] 
[stderr] 2. Review the generated `projects/<project-name>/INITIAL.md`, make any edits
[stderr]    you want, then run that named project:
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```zsh
[stderr] supervisor-run --project <project-name>
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Factory end-to-end test
[stderr] 
[stderr] Run the opt-in real-agent factory acceptance test through the generator CLI:
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```zsh
[stderr] scripts/runbookgen e2e
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] It creates a scoped game project, runs the factory, validates that it generated
[stderr] 15–20 R-series runbooks, and verifies a second run resumes accepted work.
[stderr] 
[stderr] `--project` reads that project's `INITIAL.md`, runs the reusable F-series, then
[stderr] automatically follows the generated B-series and R-series files. It keeps the
[stderr] factory's durable task history in `projects/<project-name>/.state/`, its
[stderr] versioned project configuration in `projects/<project-name>/.env`, and private
[stderr] credentials in ignored `projects/<project-name>/.secrets.env`, so a
[stderr] later `supervisor-run --project <project-name>` resumes at the first unfinished
[stderr] task and does not rerun accepted tasks. That workspace contains its normalized
[stderr] brief, specification, complete work checklist, generated B-series
[stderr] runbook-writing files, R-series product-work runbooks, and handoff
[stderr] documentation.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## See or resume project progress
[stderr] 
[stderr] From the repository root, list every named workspace and its current phase,
[stderr] accepted/pending counts, next task, and exact resume command:
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```zsh
[stderr] supervisor projects
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] Run `supervisor-run --project <project-name>` at any time to resume that
[stderr] project. Its SQLite task state makes accepted tasks skip safely and returns to
[stderr] the first unfinished task.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## What the F, B, and R series mean
[stderr] 
[stderr] The letters describe *which layer of work a runbook belongs to*, rather than a
[stderr] product feature or a required technology.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Series | Created by | Where it lives | What it does |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | `INITIAL.md` | You or `supervisor initial` | `projects/<project-name>/` | The concise source brief: what is being made, for whom, where it will run, constraints, references, and desired outcome. It gives every later stage its context. |
[stderr] | `F001`–`F016` | This repository | `runbooks/` | **Factory runbooks.** They analyse the brief, write the detailed product plan, and create the files that will write the final implementation instructions. They do not build the requested product. |
[stderr] | `B0001`, `B0002`, … | The F-series and later B dispatcher files | `projects/<slug>/authoring-runbooks/` | **Runbook-writing tasks.** A B task writes the next small set of R files. “Bounded” simply means it has a hard limit: one B task may write no more than seven R files. |
[stderr] | `R0001`, `R0002`, … | B-series runbook-writing tasks | `projects/<slug>/runbooks/` | **Product-work runbooks.** Each R file gives detailed instructions and success checks for one small piece of building, testing, reviewing, documenting, or original-asset work. Every R file explicitly declares asset metadata and provenance links to its canonical specification, catalogue records, B batch, and originating F stages. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] In short:
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```text
[stderr] your brief → F-series factory → B-series runbook writers → R-series product work
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] The hierarchy stops there: F creates the plan and the B files, B writes R files,
[stderr] and R files describe the real implementation or verification work. For example,
[stderr] a B file for an RPG's combat area might write up to seven R files: basic combat,
[stderr] damage calculation, enemies, abilities, battle UI, balancing, and tests. For a
[stderr] large product, more B files are created for the next areas instead of making one
[stderr] agent write thousands of R files at once. The Supervisor finds those later B
[stderr] files and the R files automatically, so one `--run-all` invocation continues
[stderr] until the generated collection reaches its final audit or a real review gate.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### How runbooks reference one another
[stderr] 
[stderr] Runbooks do not pass the whole project history into every later prompt. They
[stderr] use a small, explicit chain of references, while the full workspace remains
[stderr] available for targeted lookup.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | When | What is used | Why |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | `F001` | `projects/<name>/INITIAL.md` | The user’s original brief is the source of truth. F001 normalizes it but never replaces it. |
[stderr] | `F002`–`F004` | `INITIAL.md`, `PROJECT_BRIEF.md`, and the relevant earlier specification chapters | These stages refine product/domain, technical, and experience decisions. They use the documents produced by earlier F stages—not every earlier F Markdown procedure. |
[stderr] | `F005`–`F010` | The canonical `specification/` chapters and earlier planning outputs | These stages translate established decisions into dependencies, delivery areas, authoring contracts, and a safe handoff. |
[stderr] | `F011`–`F013` | The canonical specification plus the delivery/contract planning outputs | These stages create the traceability system, implementation catalogue, dependency graph, and small B-series batches. |
[stderr] | `F014`–`F016` | The catalogue, authoring manifest, quality gate, and canonical specification | These stages create B writers, validate their intended R contracts, and register the child collections that Supervisor follows. |
[stderr] | A B-series writer | Its own F013 context packet: only the assigned catalogue records, relevant specification sections, target constraints, templates, and reserved IDs | It writes no more than seven R files without needing a huge prompt or unrelated product context. |
[stderr] | An R-series task | Its own objective, dependencies, acceptance criteria, and provenance metadata | It performs one small piece of product work. It can open the linked canonical files when it needs more detail. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] Each generated R file must declare this provenance in its front matter:
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```yaml
[stderr] source_specifications: specification/03-technical-contract.md#save-state
[stderr] source_catalogue_ids: IMP-SAVE-001
[stderr] authoring_batch: B0007
[stderr] factory_stages: F003,F005,F012,F013
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] These fields answer different questions:
[stderr] 
[stderr] - `source_specifications` — which canonical requirement sections explain the work.
[stderr] - `source_catalogue_ids` — which planned implementation records the task covers.
[stderr] - `authoring_batch` — which bounded B writer created this R task.
[stderr] - `factory_stages` — which F stages established the underlying requirement.
[stderr] 
[stderr] The F and B references provide **provenance**, not a reason to use procedural
[stderr] factory Markdown as implementation requirements. The canonical specification
[stderr] and planning files remain the authoritative context. R-to-R dependencies define
[stderr] execution order; Supervisor also follows explicit `.supervisor-children/`
[stderr] registrations from the F collection to the B collection and then to the R
[stderr] collection.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### Terms used in this repository
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Term | Meaning here |
[stderr] | --- | --- |
[stderr] | **Runbook** | A Markdown work instruction with scope, steps, dependencies, and evidence required to mark it complete. |
[stderr] | **Runbook-writing task / authoring task** | A runbook whose output is more runbook Markdown files. It does not write application code. B-series files are these tasks. |
[stderr] | **Bounded** | Deliberately limited in size. A B-series task writes a maximum of seven R-series files. |
[stderr] | **Catalogue** | The complete checklist of product work that must eventually be covered: features, technical foundations, content, quality, release work, and dependencies. |
[stderr] | **Collection** | A folder of runbooks that Supervisor can run. The root F collection creates child B and R collections for one generated project. |
[stderr] | **Dispatcher** | A B-series file whose only job is to create the next B-series files when more parts of the catalogue still need R runbooks. |
[stderr] | **Contract** | The required shape of a runbook: its goal, inputs, steps, acceptance criteria, and evidence. It is not a legal agreement or an API contract. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### F-series factory stages, in detail
[stderr] 
[stderr] The F-series is deliberately product-agnostic. It uses the brief to decide what
[stderr] “complete” means for this particular product: an RPG may need story, systems,
[stderr] content, balancing, save state, and platform work; an IDE may need language
[stderr] services, debugging, extensions, and source control; an operating system may
[stderr] need kernel, hardware, security, installation, and system-management work.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Stage | What it establishes | Main output for later stages |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | `F001` | Reads `INITIAL.md`, normalizes the requested outcome, distinguishes explicit requirements from safe assumptions and unanswered decisions, and discovers the product-specific system families. | `PROJECT_BRIEF.md` and the first domain-discovery specification. |
[stderr] | `F002` | Defines the domain model, key users or actors, journeys, feature areas, release slices, and the boundaries between first release and later work. | A product map that can be divided into independently buildable areas. |
[stderr] # 10 — Specification-to-runbook authoring handoff
[stderr] 
[stderr] **Purpose:** hand the audited planning catalogue to F011–F016 without starting
[stderr] product implementation, testing, deployment, or release activity. This file is
[stderr] an authoring control record, not an implementation plan that overrides the
[stderr] [source brief](../INITIAL.md).
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Authoritative inputs and evidence locations
[stderr] 
[stderr] Read these in order. If sources conflict, preserve the source brief, record the
[stderr] conflict in the F011 decision log, and stop the affected allocation rather than
[stderr] inventing a resolution.
[stderr] 
[stderr] 1. [INITIAL.md](../INITIAL.md) is the original source of truth; the normalized
[stderr]    [product brief](../specification/01-product-brief.md) preserves its scope.
[stderr] 2. [Domain discovery](../specification/00-domain-discovery.md), the feature,
[stderr]    technical, and experience contracts (`02`–`04`) define the discovered
[stderr]    product and target constraints.
[stderr] 3. [Delivery map](05-delivery-map.md) provides the dependency order DM-01 to
[stderr]    DM-20. [Foundation](06-foundation-domain-contracts.md),
[stderr]    [feature](07-feature-experience-contracts.md), and
[stderr]    [quality](08-quality-operations-contracts.md) define FDN-01–09,
[stderr]    FEX-01–14, and QOR-01–08 ownership and acceptance evidence.
[stderr] 4. [Contract audit](09-contract-audit.md) is the current closure evidence,
[stderr]    including browser-spec ownership and retained decisions.
[stderr] 
[stderr] F011 writes canonical specification and traceability evidence under
[stderr] `specification/` (including `requirements.md`, `requirements.json`, a
[stderr] traceability matrix, decision log, platform appendix, and specification index).
[stderr] F012 writes the implementation catalogue and graph under `planning/`; F013
[stderr] writes the batch manifest there; F014 writes B-series authoring runbooks under
[stderr] `authoring-runbooks/` and their registration records; F015 writes structural
[stderr] quality-gate evidence; and F016 writes the project README and durable resume
[stderr] handoff. Generated R-series instructions and their evidence belong in
[stderr] `runbooks/` and the locations named by their final contracts. Planned test and
[stderr] browser paths are ownership reservations only until a later implementation task
[stderr] creates executable code and independent evidence.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Scope boundary and original-design rule
[stderr] 
[stderr] The P0 to author is an original, offline-capable 2D, single-player fantasy RPG:
[stderr] one town, one dungeon, a guided short campaign ending, exploration, dialogue,
[stderr] quests, party-based turn-based combat, inventory/rewards, onboarding, local
[stderr] save/load/checkpoints/recovery, settings/accessibility, responsive web, and an
[stderr] installable PWA with safe offline/update behaviour. It uses a shared core with
[stderr] only necessary web/PWA adaptations.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Do not author implementation work for additional regions, chapters, native
[stderr] apps, PWA-exclusive rules, live events, player-created/shared content, advanced
[stderr] analytics/monetisation, cloud sync, payment flows, accounts/identity,
[stderr] multiplayer, moderation, player-safety services, live operations, or
[stderr] entitlements. The brief mentions several connected capabilities, but their
[stderr] conflict with the offline P0 remains an explicit hold; no author may recast
[stderr] that hold as approved scope.
[stderr] 
[stderr] The Final Fantasy V reference permits only high-level party-based turn-based
[stderr] adventure scope. Never reproduce or request copied names, branding, assets,
[stderr] story/lore, dialogue, maps, music, layouts, menus, combat screens, interaction
[stderr] choreography, or distinctive presentation. All planned content and assets must
[stderr] be independently original, warm hand-painted fantasy with readable silhouettes
[stderr] and a restrained palette, supported by provenance records and later human
[stderr] review.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Execution order and dependencies
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Stage | Required outcome | Inputs / dependency | Cannot proceed without |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | F011 | Canonical `REQ-*`, `NFR-*`, `DEC-*`, `RISK-*`, `GATE-*` system and traceability | This handoff and all specifications/planning 05–09 | Recording, not deciding, open choices |
[stderr] | F012 | Bounded `IMP-*` catalogue, ownership, and acyclic graph | Completed F011 | Requirement traceability and named domain shards |
[stderr] | F013 | Persistent B/R allocation ledger and context packets | Completed F012 | Unique immutable IDs and dependency-safe batches of at most seven R contracts |
[stderr] | F014 | Bootstrap B-series/dispatcher collection and child registrations | Completed F013 | Valid manifest, bounded wave, and existing-ID collision checks |
[stderr] | F015 | Structural/traceability quality gate and recovery procedure | F014 output plus manifest | Passing ownership, metadata, dependency, asset, and browser-path checks |
[stderr] | F016 | Project README, registrations, evidence map, and resume handoff | F015 quality evidence | Explicit holds and safe resume points retained |
[stderr] 
[stderr] Within later implementation contracts, preserve the DM order: select and
[stderr] bootstrap the framework before core/state/content; establish persistence and
[stderr] recovery before session/UI; complete campaign rules before their UI; then input
[stderr] and accessibility, PWA lifecycle, release hardening, quality evidence, and
[stderr] documentation. Parallel authoring is allowed only for contracts whose declared
[stderr] dependencies are already satisfied and whose files/data/browser paths do not
[stderr] overlap.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Decision gates, approvals, and stop conditions
[stderr] 
[stderr] The following gates require a human or responsible external owner before a
[stderr] dependent contract is allocated as ready: engine/framework and package manager;
[stderr] CI runtime; browser storage API/quota; browser/device/assistive-tech and
[stderr] network matrix; numeric performance budget; host/CDN and service-worker rollout;
[stderr] content quantities, party size, balance, locale, audio and remapping scope;
[stderr] font/contrast targets; diagnostics provider/transport, retention and privacy;
[stderr] and ratings, age, regional, legal, support, store, or certification policy.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Stop the batch authoring system and mark the item blocked—not failed or
[stderr] implicitly approved—when any of these occurs:
[stderr] 
[stderr] - an open decision changes scope, privacy/security, cost, legal obligations,
[stderr]   connected services, or delivery commitments;
[stderr] - a requirement lacks a source, owner, verification approach, or safe bounded
[stderr]   contract; a dependency is cyclic or ownership conflicts;
[stderr] - an R/B/asset ID, output path, browser test path, or accepted contract would
[stderr]   be overwritten, renumbered, duplicated, or ambiguously owned;
[stderr] - a request entails credentials, paid services, account creation, store action,
[stderr]   deployment, publishing, release approval, or real-device/certification access;
[stderr] - evidence would require claiming that planned paths, generated runbooks, or
[stderr]   structural validation prove product implementation or release readiness.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Safe authoring-agent configuration
[stderr] 
[stderr] Use a workspace-scoped authoring agent with no secrets, deployments, payments,
[stderr] publishing, external account actions, or authority to approve gates. Give each
[stderr] agent only its manifest context packet, the source documents it cites, its
[stderr] assigned immutable IDs, and its declared output paths. It may create Markdown,
[stderr] JSON manifests, validation instructions, and child-registration files only in
[stderr] this project workspace; it must not create product source, generated game
[stderr] assets, tests that purport to validate a product, or release artefacts in this
[stderr] F011–F016 phase.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Every authoring task records source links, requirement/contract IDs,
[stderr] dependencies, owned outputs, unresolved questions, gate status, and structural
[stderr] evidence. It checkpoints after each bounded batch, retains accepted IDs and
[stderr] manifest counters, and resumes from the next unaccepted item. F015 is the
[stderr] structural evidence gate: it validates metadata, traceability, dependency
[stderr] closure, output ownership, asset assessment, and browser-path uniqueness. Any
[stderr] later executable test, browser result, visual review, accessibility assessment,
[stderr] or release evidence must be stored at the paths named by its implementation
[stderr] contract and remains outside this authoring handoff.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Assumptions and risks carried forward
[stderr] 
[stderr] Safe assumptions are limited to an offline-first local campaign, original
[stderr] content/UI, shared core with necessary target adaptations, local durable saves
[stderr] where browser storage allows, visibly degraded connected features if ever
[stderr] approved, and privacy-minimised diagnostics. They are guardrails, not stack,
[stderr] vendor, backend, account, retention, performance, accessibility, or support
[stderr] promises.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Key risks are unresolved connected-scope conflict; storage/quota and update
[stderr] loss hazards; unsupported browser/device or assistive-tech assumptions;
[stderr] unapproved performance targets; original-design/provenance failure; and
[stderr] manifest/ID drift across resumed authoring waves. Mitigate each by retaining
[stderr] the named gate, keeping save/cache responsibilities separated, requiring
[stderr] provenance and human creative review, and using the F013 ledger plus F015
[stderr] quality gate before dispatching further work.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Readiness statement
[stderr] 
[stderr] The catalogue is ready for **batch authoring only**: audited inputs identify
[stderr] the P0 boundary, contract namespaces, delivery dependencies, evidence owners,
[stderr] and unresolved decision gates. F011–F016 may now formalise, shard, allocate,
[stderr] validate, and hand off implementation instructions. This statement does not
[stderr] select a technology, authorize any gate, create implementation or release
[stderr] work, or assert that the game has been built, tested, or approved for release.
